    objectives: "• Multiply fractions and mixed numbers; interpret area models.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Multiplying Fractions\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 2 of 11 · Arithmetic with Rational Numbers · Lesson 3*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** multiply fractions and mixed numbers; interpret area models.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout calculating yet, estimate which is greater: 7/3 of 22, or -10% of 22? Jot a one-sentence guess.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 2 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Multiplying Fractions** — sits inside arithmetic with rational numbers. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine an East Texas trail hike near Tyler. Related quantities show up as 7 and 3 (and sometimes a third measure 22).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 7 and 3. One useful related value is 10; another is a scaled pair such as 14 to 6 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for an East Texas trail hike near Tyler?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 7 to 3. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 51, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Multiplying Fractions” but switch the context to a church youth group picnic. Use numbers -1, 17, and 22.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** combining unlike terms (for example, 3x + 2 becomes 5x).\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nFraction and decimal arithmetic must stay connected to meaning — area models and place-value charts prevent “calculator-only” habits.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 7 and 3. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 7 and 3 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers -1 and 22. Explain why your representation matches the situation (an East Texas trail hike near Tyler).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “21.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. A garden plot is 1/3 of a full bed wide and 3/5 of a full bed long. What fraction of a full bed’s area is the plot? Multiply and simplify.\n2. A garden plot is 2/4 of a full bed wide and -1/7 of a full bed long. What fraction of a full bed’s area is the plot? Multiply and simplify.\n3. A garden plot is 2/5 of a full bed wide and 2/6 of a full bed long. What fraction of a full bed’s area is the plot? Multiply and simplify.\n4. A garden plot is 3/6 of a full bed wide and 3/8 of a full bed long. What fraction of a full bed’s area is the plot? Multiply and simplify.\n5. A garden plot is 1/2 of a full bed wide and 2/5 of a full bed long. What fraction of a full bed’s area is the plot? Multiply and simplify.\n6. A garden plot is 1/3 of a full bed wide and 3/4 of a full bed long. What fraction of a full bed’s area is the plot? Multiply and simplify.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. 1/3 × 3/5 = 3/15 = 1/5.\n2. 2/4 × -1/7 = -2/28 = -1/14.\n3. 2/5 × 2/6 = 4/30 = 2/15.\n4. 3/6 × 3/8 = 9/48 = 3/16.\n5. 1/2 × 2/5 = 2/10 = 1/5.\n6. 1/3 × 3/4 = 3/12 = 1/4.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Multiplying Fractions” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to combining unlike terms (for example, 3x…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Multiplying Fractions.” Use one concrete numeric example with 7 and 3. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
