    id: "ppg6m1346a92b50b1cb896407",
    title: "Dividing by a Fraction",
    description: "Use keep-change-flip with meaning; check with multiplication.",
    objectives: "• Use keep-change-flip with meaning; check with multiplication.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Dividing by a Fraction\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 2 of 11 · Arithmetic with Rational Numbers · Lesson 5*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** use keep-change-flip with meaning; check with multiplication.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout calculating yet, estimate which is greater: 3/5 of 25, or 90% of 25? Jot a one-sentence guess.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 2 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Dividing by a Fraction** — sits inside arithmetic with rational numbers. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a scholarship fundraising bake sale. Related quantities show up as 3 and 5 (and sometimes a third measure 25).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 3 and 5. One useful related value is 8; another is a scaled pair such as 6 to 10 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a scholarship fundraising bake sale?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 3 to 5. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 15, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Dividing by a Fraction” but switch the context to a library reading challenge. Use numbers 9, 20, and 25.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** calling a non-statistical question 'statistical' because it uses a number.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nFraction and decimal arithmetic must stay connected to meaning — area models and place-value charts prevent “calculator-only” habits.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 3 and 5. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 3 and 5 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 9 and 25. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a scholarship fundraising bake sale).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “15.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. How many 2/3-cup servings are in -1 cups of mix? Use keep-change-flip and check with multiplication.\n2. How many 2/4-cup servings are in 0 cups of mix? Use keep-change-flip and check with multiplication.\n3. How many 3/5-cup servings are in 1 cups of mix? Use keep-change-flip and check with multiplication.\n4. How many 5/6-cup servings are in 4 cups of mix? Use keep-change-flip and check with multiplication.\n5. How many 1/2-cup servings are in 4 cups of mix? Use keep-change-flip and check with multiplication.\n6. How many 2/3-cup servings are in 5 cups of mix? Use keep-change-flip and check with multiplication.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. -1 ÷ 2/3 = -1 × 3/2 = -1.5. Check: servings × 2/3 = -1.\n2. 0 ÷ 2/4 = 0 × 4/2 = 0. Check: servings × 2/4 = 0.\n3. 1 ÷ 3/5 = 1 × 5/3 = 1.6666666666666667. Check: servings × 3/5 = 1.\n4. 4 ÷ 5/6 = 4 × 6/5 = 4.8. Check: servings × 5/6 = 4.\n5. 4 ÷ 1/2 = 4 × 2/1 = 8. Check: servings × 1/2 = 4.\n6. 5 ÷ 2/3 = 5 × 3/2 = 7.5. Check: servings × 2/3 = 5.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Dividing by a Fraction” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to calling a non-statistical question 'statistical' because…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Dividing by a Fraction.” Use one concrete numeric example with 3 and 5. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
    order: 15,
    durationMin: 40,
