    objectives: "• Convert fluently; choose the form that fits the problem.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Mixed Numbers and Improper Fractions\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 2 of 11 · Arithmetic with Rational Numbers · Lesson 6*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** convert fluently; choose the form that fits the problem.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nSketch a tiny diagram (table, number line, or shape) that might help with: \"Convert fluently; choose the form that fits the problem.\"\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 2 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Mixed Numbers and Improper Fractions** — sits inside arithmetic with rational numbers. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a science fair supply run. Related quantities show up as 3 and 6 (and sometimes a third measure 32).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 3 and 6. One useful related value is 9; another is a scaled pair such as 6 to 12 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a science fair supply run?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 3 to 6. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 16, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Mixed Numbers and Improper Fractions” but switch the context to a Saturday soccer tournament. Use numbers 6, 7, and 32.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** combining unlike terms (for example, 3x + 2 becomes 5x).\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nFraction and decimal arithmetic must stay connected to meaning — area models and place-value charts prevent “calculator-only” habits.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 3 and 6. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 3 and 6 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 6 and 32. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a science fair supply run).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “18.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Convert 2 1/3 to an improper fraction. Convert 10/3 to a mixed number. Context: measuring for a family trip on I-20.\n2. Convert 3 1/4 to an improper fraction. Convert 17/4 to a mixed number. Context: measuring for a scholarship bake sale.\n3. Convert 1 1/5 to an improper fraction. Convert 11/5 to a mixed number. Context: measuring for an East Texas trail hike.\n4. Convert 2 3/6 to an improper fraction. Convert 21/6 to a mixed number. Context: measuring for Prosper Prep basketball practice.\n5. Convert 3 1/2 to an improper fraction. Convert 9/2 to a mixed number. Context: measuring for a library reading challenge.\n6. Convert 1 2/3 to an improper fraction. Convert 8/3 to a mixed number. Context: measuring for a youth-group picnic.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. 2 1/3 = 7/3. 10/3 = 3 1/3 (or 3.3333333333333335 if num1=0).\n2. 3 1/4 = 13/4. 17/4 = 4 1/4 (or 4.25 if num1=0).\n3. 1 1/5 = 6/5. 11/5 = 2 1/5 (or 2.2 if num1=0).\n4. 2 3/6 = 15/6. 21/6 = 3 3/6 (or 3.5 if num1=0).\n5. 3 1/2 = 7/2. 9/2 = 4 1/2 (or 4.5 if num1=0).\n6. 1 2/3 = 5/3. 8/3 = 2 2/3 (or 2.6666666666666665 if num1=0).\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Mixed Numbers and Improper Fractions” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to combining unlike terms (for example, 3x…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Mixed Numbers and Improper Fractions.” Use one concrete numeric example with 3 and 6. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
