    objectives: "• Read, write, and compare decimals through thousandths.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Decimal Place Value\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 2 of 11 · Arithmetic with Rational Numbers · Lesson 7*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** read, write, and compare decimals through thousandths.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nSketch a tiny diagram (table, number line, or shape) that might help with: \"Read, write, and compare decimals through thousandths.\"\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 2 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Decimal Place Value** — sits inside arithmetic with rational numbers. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a family road trip on I-20. Related quantities show up as 9 and -3 (and sometimes a third measure 28).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 9 and -3. One useful related value is 6; another is a scaled pair such as 18 to -6 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a family road trip on I-20?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 9 to -3. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near -84, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Decimal Place Value” but switch the context to a church youth group picnic. Use numbers 1, 23, and 28.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** forgetting that height must be perpendicular to the chosen base.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nFraction and decimal arithmetic must stay connected to meaning — area models and place-value charts prevent “calculator-only” habits.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 9 and -3. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 9 and -3 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 1 and 28. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a family road trip on I-20).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “-27.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Compare 8.005 and 6.06. Which is greater? Write both to thousandths and explain using place value (for scores at a science-fair supply run).\n2. Compare 6.999 and 6.05. Which is greater? Write both to thousandths and explain using place value (for scores at Prosper Prep basketball practice).\n3. Compare 7.002 and 7.24. Which is greater? Write both to thousandths and explain using place value (for scores at a library reading challenge).\n4. Compare 8.007 and 10.23. Which is greater? Write both to thousandths and explain using place value (for scores at a scholarship bake sale).\n5. Compare 8.010 and 11.22. Which is greater? Write both to thousandths and explain using place value (for scores at an East Texas trail hike).\n6. Compare 12.012 and 11.05. Which is greater? Write both to thousandths and explain using place value (for scores at Prosper Prep basketball practice).\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. 8.005 vs 6.060. Greater: 8.005.\n2. 6.999 vs 6.050. Greater: 6.999.\n3. 7.002 vs 7.240. Greater: 7.240.\n4. 8.007 vs 10.230. Greater: 10.230.\n5. 8.010 vs 11.220. Greater: 11.220.\n6. 12.012 vs 11.050. Greater: 12.012.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Decimal Place Value” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to forgetting that height must be perpendicular…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Decimal Place Value.” Use one concrete numeric example with 9 and -3. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
