    objectives: "• Move fluently between fractions and decimals in context.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Fraction–Decimal Connections\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 2 of 11 · Arithmetic with Rational Numbers · Lesson 10*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** move fluently between fractions and decimals in context.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nSketch a tiny diagram (table, number line, or shape) that might help with: \"Move fluently between fractions and decimals in context.\"\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 2 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Fraction–Decimal Connections** — sits inside arithmetic with rational numbers. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a band concert ticket table. Related quantities show up as 3 and 0 (and sometimes a third measure 48).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 3 and 0. One useful related value is 3; another is a scaled pair such as 6 to 0 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a band concert ticket table?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 3 to 0. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near Infinity, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Fraction–Decimal Connections” but switch the context to a scholarship fundraising bake sale. Use numbers 5, 23, and 48.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** treating percent as 'per ten' instead of 'per hundred'.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nFraction and decimal arithmetic must stay connected to meaning — area models and place-value charts prevent “calculator-only” habits.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 3 and 0. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 3 and 0 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 5 and 48. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a band concert ticket table).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “0.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Write 1/3 as a decimal (divide) and as a percent. Then write 0.14 as a fraction in simplest form. Context: a family trip on I-20.\n2. Write 3/4 as a decimal (divide) and as a percent. Then write 0.5-2 as a fraction in simplest form. Context: a scholarship bake sale.\n3. Write 2/5 as a decimal (divide) and as a percent. Then write 0.51 as a fraction in simplest form. Context: an East Texas trail hike.\n4. Write 5/6 as a decimal (divide) and as a percent. Then write 0.03 as a fraction in simplest form. Context: Prosper Prep basketball practice.\n5. Write 1/2 as a decimal (divide) and as a percent. Then write 0.07 as a fraction in simplest form. Context: a library reading challenge.\n6. Write 2/3 as a decimal (divide) and as a percent. Then write 0.40 as a fraction in simplest form. Context: a youth-group picnic.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. 1/3 = 0.3333 = 33.33%. Convert 0.14 by place value and simplify.\n2. 3/4 = 0.7500 = 75.00%. Convert 0.5-2 by place value and simplify.\n3. 2/5 = 0.4000 = 40.00%. Convert 0.51 by place value and simplify.\n4. 5/6 = 0.8333 = 83.33%. Convert 0.03 by place value and simplify.\n5. 1/2 = 0.5000 = 50.00%. Convert 0.07 by place value and simplify.\n6. 2/3 = 0.6667 = 66.67%. Convert 0.40 by place value and simplify.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Fraction–Decimal Connections” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to treating percent as 'per ten' instead…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Fraction–Decimal Connections.” Use one concrete numeric example with 3 and 0. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
