    description: "Apply unit rates to speed, unit price, and work contexts.",
    objectives: "• Apply unit rates to speed, unit price, and work contexts.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Speed, Price, and Work Rates\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 3 of 11 · Rates and Percentages · Lesson 2*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** apply unit rates to speed, unit price, and work contexts.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nSketch a tiny diagram (table, number line, or shape) that might help with: \"Apply unit rates to speed, unit price, and work contexts.\"\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 3 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Speed, Price, and Work Rates** — sits inside rates and percentages. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a school garden plot. Related quantities show up as 7 and 6 (and sometimes a third measure 47).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 7 and 6. One useful related value is 13; another is a scaled pair such as 14 to 12 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a school garden plot?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 7 to 6. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 55, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Speed, Price, and Work Rates” but switch the context to a library reading challenge. Use numbers 6, 22, and 47.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** switching the order of a ratio (comparing B to A when the question asked A to B).\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nPercents are rates “per 100.” Unit rates from earlier lessons transfer directly.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 7 and 6. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 7 and 6 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 6 and 47. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a school garden plot).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “42.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. A bus covers 48 miles in 3 hours for a trip near a scholarship bake sale. What is the unit rate in mph? How far at that rate in 5 hours?\n2. A bus covers 69 miles in 4 hours for a trip near a family trip on I-20. What is the unit rate in mph? How far at that rate in 6 hours?\n3. A bus covers 50 miles in 2 hours for a trip near the school garden. What is the unit rate in mph? How far at that rate in 4 hours?\n4. A bus covers 31 miles in 3 hours for a trip near a band concert ticket table. What is the unit rate in mph? How far at that rate in 5 hours?\n5. A bus covers 52 miles in 4 hours for a trip near a Saturday soccer tournament. What is the unit rate in mph? How far at that rate in 6 hours?\n6. A bus covers 33 miles in 2 hours for a trip near a science-fair supply run. What is the unit rate in mph? How far at that rate in 4 hours?\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. 16.00 mph. In 5 h: 80.00 miles.\n2. 17.25 mph. In 6 h: 103.50 miles.\n3. 25.00 mph. In 4 h: 100.00 miles.\n4. 10.33 mph. In 5 h: 51.67 miles.\n5. 13.00 mph. In 6 h: 78.00 miles.\n6. 16.50 mph. In 4 h: 66.00 miles.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Speed, Price, and Work Rates” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to switching the order of a ratio…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Speed, Price, and Work Rates.” Use one concrete numeric example with 7 and 6. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
