    id: "ppg6mb6e029ef066c9e42cc35",
    title: "Complex Rate Tables",
    description: "Extend rate tables for multi-step planning problems.",
    objectives: "• Extend rate tables for multi-step planning problems.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Complex Rate Tables\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 3 of 11 · Rates and Percentages · Lesson 3*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** extend rate tables for multi-step planning problems.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout calculating yet, estimate which is greater: 6/2 of 33, or 30% of 33? Jot a one-sentence guess.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 3 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Complex Rate Tables** — sits inside rates and percentages. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a church youth group picnic. Related quantities show up as 6 and 2 (and sometimes a third measure 33).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 6 and 2. One useful related value is 8; another is a scaled pair such as 12 to 4 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a church youth group picnic?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 6 to 2. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 99, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Complex Rate Tables” but switch the context to a science fair supply run. Use numbers 3, 8, and 33.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** doing operations left-to-right while ignoring parentheses or exponents.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nPercents are rates “per 100.” Unit rates from earlier lessons transfer directly.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 6 and 2. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 6 and 2 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 3 and 33. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a church youth group picnic).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “12.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. A print shop makes 4 posters every 0 minutes. Build a rate table for 1, 2, and 5 “blocks” of 0 minutes. How many posters in 0 minutes?\n2. A print shop makes 11 posters every 0 minutes. Build a rate table for 1, 2, and 5 “blocks” of 0 minutes. How many posters in 0 minutes?\n3. A print shop makes 13 posters every 7 minutes. Build a rate table for 1, 2, and 5 “blocks” of 7 minutes. How many posters in 35 minutes?\n4. A print shop makes 10 posters every 4 minutes. Build a rate table for 1, 2, and 5 “blocks” of 4 minutes. How many posters in 20 minutes?\n5. A print shop makes 12 posters every 3 minutes. Build a rate table for 1, 2, and 5 “blocks” of 3 minutes. How many posters in 15 minutes?\n6. A print shop makes 10 posters every 3 minutes. Build a rate table for 1, 2, and 5 “blocks” of 3 minutes. How many posters in 15 minutes?\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. Blocks: (0 min → 4), (0 → 8), (0 → 20). In 0 min: 20 posters.\n2. Blocks: (0 min → 11), (0 → 22), (0 → 55). In 0 min: 55 posters.\n3. Blocks: (7 min → 13), (14 → 26), (35 → 65). In 35 min: 65 posters.\n4. Blocks: (4 min → 10), (8 → 20), (20 → 50). In 20 min: 50 posters.\n5. Blocks: (3 min → 12), (6 → 24), (15 → 60). In 15 min: 60 posters.\n6. Blocks: (3 min → 10), (6 → 20), (15 → 50). In 15 min: 50 posters.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Complex Rate Tables” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to doing operations left-to-right while ignoring parentheses…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Complex Rate Tables.” Use one concrete numeric example with 6 and 2. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
