    id: "ppg6m091444c4041475c9c62c",
    title: "Rates & Percents Unit Review",
    description: "Mixed practice connecting rates, ratios, and percents.",
    objectives: "• Mixed practice connecting rates, ratios, and percents.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Rates & Percents Unit Review\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 3 of 11 · Rates and Percentages · Lesson 10*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** mixed practice connecting rates, ratios, and percents.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nLook at these two quantities from a Saturday soccer tournament: 3 and 7. What is one mathematical question you could ask about how they relate?\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 3 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Rates & Percents Unit Review** — sits inside rates and percentages. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a Saturday soccer tournament. Related quantities show up as 3 and 7 (and sometimes a third measure 17).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 3 and 7. One useful related value is 10; another is a scaled pair such as 6 to 14 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a Saturday soccer tournament?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 3 to 7. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 7, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Rates & Percents Unit Review” but switch the context to a library reading challenge. Use numbers 8, 12, and 17.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** adding denominators when multiplying fractions.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nPercents are rates “per 100.” Unit rates from earlier lessons transfer directly.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 3 and 7. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 3 and 7 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 8 and 17. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a Saturday soccer tournament).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “21.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Mixed rates/percents (Rates & Percents Unit Review): Unit rate for 55 miles in 5 hours; then find 50% of 30. Context: a scholarship bake sale.\n2. Mixed rates/percents (Rates & Percents Unit Review): Unit rate for 45 miles in 5 hours; then find 220% of 47. Context: a library reading challenge.\n3. Mixed rates/percents (Rates & Percents Unit Review): Unit rate for 44 miles in 4 hours; then find 230% of 28. Context: Prosper Prep basketball practice.\n4. Mixed rates/percents (Rates & Percents Unit Review): Unit rate for 8 miles in 1 hours; then find 80% of 13. Context: a band concert ticket table.\n5. Mixed rates/percents (Rates & Percents Unit Review): Unit rate for 80 miles in 8 hours; then find 90% of 34. Context: a Saturday soccer tournament.\n6. Mixed rates/percents (Rates & Percents Unit Review): Unit rate for 136 miles in 8 hours; then find 60% of 11. Context: a family trip on I-20.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. Unit rate 11.00 mph. 50% of 30 = 15.\n2. Unit rate 9.00 mph. 220% of 47 = 103.4.\n3. Unit rate 11.00 mph. 230% of 28 = 64.39999999999999.\n4. Unit rate 8.00 mph. 80% of 13 = 10.4.\n5. Unit rate 10.00 mph. 90% of 34 = 30.6.\n6. Unit rate 17.00 mph. 60% of 11 = 6.6.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Rates & Percents Unit Review” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to adding denominators when multiplying fractions…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Rates & Percents Unit Review.” Use one concrete numeric example with 3 and 7. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
