    content: "# Order of Operations Foundations\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 4 of 11 · Exponents and Order of Operations · Lesson 3*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** apply parentheses, exponents, multiply/divide, add/subtract consistently.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nLook at these two quantities from a family road trip on I-20: 8 and -2. What is one mathematical question you could ask about how they relate?\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 4 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Order of Operations Foundations** — sits inside exponents and order of operations. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a family road trip on I-20. Related quantities show up as 8 and -2 (and sometimes a third measure 16).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 8 and -2. One useful related value is 6; another is a scaled pair such as 16 to -4 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a family road trip on I-20?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 8 to -2. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near -64, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Order of Operations Foundations” but switch the context to a school garden plot. Use numbers 1, 11, and 16.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** combining unlike terms (for example, 3x + 2 becomes 5x).\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nStatistics begins with questions that anticipate variability — displays and measures of center come after the question is clear.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 8 and -2. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 8 and -2 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 1 and 16. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a family road trip on I-20).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “-16.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Evaluate 3 + 9 × 8^2 − 8. Show the order (×/÷ and exponents before +/−).\n2. Evaluate 4 + 11 × 7^2 − 9. Show the order (×/÷ and exponents before +/−).\n3. Evaluate 5 + 13 × 6^2 − 10. Show the order (×/÷ and exponents before +/−).\n4. Evaluate 6 + 7 × 7^2 − 13. Show the order (×/÷ and exponents before +/−).\n5. Evaluate 2 + 9 × 14^2 − 13. Show the order (×/÷ and exponents before +/−).\n6. Evaluate 3 + 11 × 12^2 − 14. Show the order (×/÷ and exponents before +/−).\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. Exponents first: 8^2 = 64. Then ×: 576. Then +/−: 571.\n2. Exponents first: 7^2 = 49. Then ×: 539. Then +/−: 534.\n3. Exponents first: 6^2 = 36. Then ×: 468. Then +/−: 463.\n4. Exponents first: 7^2 = 49. Then ×: 343. Then +/−: 336.\n5. Exponents first: 14^2 = 196. Then ×: 1764. Then +/−: 1753.\n6. Exponents first: 12^2 = 144. Then ×: 1584. Then +/−: 1573.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Order of Operations Foundations” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to combining unlike terms (for example, 3x…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Order of Operations Foundations.” Use one concrete numeric example with 8 and -2. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
