    id: "ppg6mf40debd88876a9170582",
    title: "Expressions with Grouping Symbols",
    description: "Use parentheses and brackets to control evaluation order.",
    objectives: "• Use parentheses and brackets to control evaluation order.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Expressions with Grouping Symbols\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 4 of 11 · Exponents and Order of Operations · Lesson 4*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** use parentheses and brackets to control evaluation order.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout calculating yet, estimate which is greater: 9/-3 of 32, or -10% of 32? Jot a one-sentence guess.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 4 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Expressions with Grouping Symbols** — sits inside exponents and order of operations. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a band concert ticket table. Related quantities show up as 9 and -3 (and sometimes a third measure 32).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 9 and -3. One useful related value is 6; another is a scaled pair such as 18 to -6 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a band concert ticket table?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 9 to -3. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near -96, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Expressions with Grouping Symbols” but switch the context to a Saturday soccer tournament. Use numbers -1, 7, and 32.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** treating percent as 'per ten' instead of 'per hundred'.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nStatistics begins with questions that anticipate variability — displays and measures of center come after the question is clear.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 9 and -3. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 9 and -3 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers -1 and 32. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a band concert ticket table).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “-27.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Evaluate (3 + 8) × 3 − 5^2 and compare to 3 + 8 × 3 − 5^2. Why do they differ?\n2. Evaluate (4 + 10) × 2 − 6^2 and compare to 4 + 10 × 2 − 6^2. Why do they differ?\n3. Evaluate (5 + 12) × 1 − 7^2 and compare to 5 + 12 × 1 − 7^2. Why do they differ?\n4. Evaluate (6 + 6) × 2 − 3^2 and compare to 6 + 6 × 2 − 3^2. Why do they differ?\n5. Evaluate (2 + 8) × 1 − 4^2 and compare to 2 + 8 × 1 − 4^2. Why do they differ?\n6. Evaluate (3 + 10) × 7 − 4^2 and compare to 3 + 10 × 7 − 4^2. Why do they differ?\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. With grouping: (11)×3 − 25 = 8. Without: 2. Parentheses change order.\n2. With grouping: (14)×2 − 36 = -8. Without: -12. Parentheses change order.\n3. With grouping: (17)×1 − 49 = -32. Without: -32. Parentheses change order.\n4. With grouping: (12)×2 − 9 = 15. Without: 9. Parentheses change order.\n5. With grouping: (10)×1 − 16 = -6. Without: -6. Parentheses change order.\n6. With grouping: (13)×7 − 16 = 75. Without: 57. Parentheses change order.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Expressions with Grouping Symbols” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to treating percent as 'per ten' instead…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Expressions with Grouping Symbols.” Use one concrete numeric example with 9 and -3. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
