    content: "# Writing Expressions with Exponents\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 4 of 11 · Exponents and Order of Operations · Lesson 6*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** translate verbal phrases that include powers.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nLook at these two quantities from an East Texas trail hike near Tyler: 10 and 2. What is one mathematical question you could ask about how they relate?\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 4 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Writing Expressions with Exponents** — sits inside exponents and order of operations. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine an East Texas trail hike near Tyler. Related quantities show up as 10 and 2 (and sometimes a third measure 48).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 10 and 2. One useful related value is 12; another is a scaled pair such as 20 to 4 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for an East Texas trail hike near Tyler?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 10 to 2. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 240, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Writing Expressions with Exponents” but switch the context to a Prosper Prep basketball practice. Use numbers 4, 23, and 48.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** plotting (y, x) instead of (x, y) on the coordinate plane.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nStatistics begins with questions that anticipate variability — displays and measures of center come after the question is clear.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 10 and 2. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 10 and 2 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 4 and 48. Explain why your representation matches the situation (an East Texas trail hike near Tyler).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “20.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Translate: “the square of a number n, plus 4” and “4 times the cube of a side length s.”\n2. Translate: “the square of a number n, plus 6” and “3 times the cube of a side length s.”\n3. Translate: “the square of a number n, plus 8” and “2 times the cube of a side length s.”\n4. Translate: “the square of a number n, plus 11” and “3 times the cube of a side length s.”\n5. Translate: “the square of a number n, plus 13” and “2 times the cube of a side length s.”\n6. Translate: “the square of a number n, plus 15” and “8 times the cube of a side length s.”\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. n^2 + 4; 4s^3.\n2. n^2 + 6; 3s^3.\n3. n^2 + 8; 2s^3.\n4. n^2 + 11; 3s^3.\n5. n^2 + 13; 2s^3.\n6. n^2 + 15; 8s^3.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Writing Expressions with Exponents” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to plotting (y, x) instead of (x,…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Writing Expressions with Exponents.” Use one concrete numeric example with 10 and 2. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
