    objectives: "• Place fractions and decimals on both sides of zero.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Rational Numbers Beyond Integers\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 5 of 11 · Negative Numbers · Lesson 5*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** place fractions and decimals on both sides of zero.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout calculating yet, estimate which is greater: 11/9 of 18, or 90% of 18? Jot a one-sentence guess.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 5 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Rational Numbers Beyond Integers** — sits inside negative numbers. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a science fair supply run. Related quantities show up as 11 and 9 (and sometimes a third measure 18).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 11 and 9. One useful related value is 20; another is a scaled pair such as 22 to 18 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a science fair supply run?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 11 to 9. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 22, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Rational Numbers Beyond Integers” but switch the context to a Prosper Prep basketball practice. Use numbers 9, 13, and 18.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** plotting (y, x) instead of (x, y) on the coordinate plane.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nNegatives extend the number line; absolute value is distance, not a magic “drop the sign” button.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 11 and 9. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 11 and 9 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 9 and 18. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a science fair supply run).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “99.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Place −10/8, -7, and 0.10 on a number line sketch. Which is least?\n2. Place −10/11, -10, and 0.11 on a number line sketch. Which is least?\n3. Place −10/7, -6, and 0.13 on a number line sketch. Which is least?\n4. Place −10/10, -9, and 0.14 on a number line sketch. Which is least?\n5. Place −10/13, -12, and 0.15 on a number line sketch. Which is least?\n6. Place −10/17, -16, and 0.16 on a number line sketch. Which is least?\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. Compare decimal values: −1.250, -7, 1.0. Least = most negative.\n2. Compare decimal values: −0.909, -10, 1.1. Least = most negative.\n3. Compare decimal values: −1.429, -6, 1.3. Least = most negative.\n4. Compare decimal values: −1.000, -9, 1.4. Least = most negative.\n5. Compare decimal values: −0.769, -12, 1.5. Least = most negative.\n6. Compare decimal values: −0.588, -16, 1.6. Least = most negative.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Rational Numbers Beyond Integers” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to plotting (y, x) instead of (x,…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Rational Numbers Beyond Integers.” Use one concrete numeric example with 11 and 9. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
