    id: "ppg6m7b6f039067e575845ca3",
    title: "One-Step Addition and Subtraction Equations",
    description: "Solve with inverse operations; check solutions.",
    objectives: "• Solve with inverse operations; check solutions.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# One-Step Addition and Subtraction Equations\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 7 of 11 · Equations & Inequalities · Lesson 2*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** solve with inverse operations; check solutions.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nLook at these two quantities from a school garden plot: 3 and -3. What is one mathematical question you could ask about how they relate?\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 7 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **One-Step Addition and Subtraction Equations** — sits inside equations & inequalities. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a school garden plot. Related quantities show up as 3 and -3 (and sometimes a third measure 37).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 3 and -3. One useful related value is 0; another is a scaled pair such as 6 to -6 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a school garden plot?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 3 to -3. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near -37, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “One-Step Addition and Subtraction Equations” but switch the context to a family road trip on I-20. Use numbers 0, 12, and 37.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** adding denominators when multiplying fractions.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nExpressions name patterns; equations assert balance. Keep that distinction sharp.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 3 and -3. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 3 and -3 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 0 and 37. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a school garden plot).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “-9.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Solve x + 12 = 21 and y − 9 = 12. Check each solution.\n2. Solve x + 7 = 18 and y − 11 = 7. Check each solution.\n3. Solve x + 7 = 21 and y − 14 = 7. Check each solution.\n4. Solve x + 11 = 19 and y − 8 = 11. Check each solution.\n5. Solve x + 11 = 23 and y − 12 = 11. Check each solution.\n6. Solve x + 15 = 29 and y − 14 = 15. Check each solution.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. x = 9; check 9+12=21. y = 21; check 21−9=12.\n2. x = 11; check 11+7=18. y = 18; check 18−11=7.\n3. x = 14; check 14+7=21. y = 21; check 21−14=7.\n4. x = 8; check 8+11=19. y = 19; check 19−8=11.\n5. x = 12; check 12+11=23. y = 23; check 23−12=11.\n6. x = 14; check 14+15=29. y = 29; check 29−14=15.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “One-Step Addition and Subtraction Equations” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to adding denominators when multiplying fractions…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “One-Step Addition and Subtraction Equations.” Use one concrete numeric example with 3 and -3. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
