    objectives: "• Relate perimeter and area; avoid mixing them up.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Polygons and Perimeter Connections\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 8 of 11 · Plane Figures · Lesson 7*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** relate perimeter and area; avoid mixing them up.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nLook at these two quantities from a school garden plot: 7 and 4. What is one mathematical question you could ask about how they relate?\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 8 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Polygons and Perimeter Connections** — sits inside plane figures. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a school garden plot. Related quantities show up as 7 and 4 (and sometimes a third measure 45).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 7 and 4. One useful related value is 11; another is a scaled pair such as 14 to 8 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a school garden plot?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 7 to 4. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 79, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Polygons and Perimeter Connections” but switch the context to an East Texas trail hike near Tyler. Use numbers 5, 20, and 45.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** dropping a negative sign when comparing signed numbers.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nGeometry measures need correct units and clear diagrams. Algebraic expressions often summarize geometric patterns.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 7 and 4. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 7 and 4 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 5 and 45. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a school garden plot).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “28.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. A rectangle is 11 by -1. Find perimeter and area. A student says “area is 20.” What did they confuse?\n2. A rectangle is 15 by 1. Find perimeter and area. A student says “area is 32.” What did they confuse?\n3. A rectangle is 15 by 4. Find perimeter and area. A student says “area is 38.” What did they confuse?\n4. A rectangle is 10 by 6. Find perimeter and area. A student says “area is 32.” What did they confuse?\n5. A rectangle is 10 by 10. Find perimeter and area. A student says “area is 40.” What did they confuse?\n6. A rectangle is 14 by 4. Find perimeter and area. A student says “area is 36.” What did they confuse?\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. P = 20; A = -11. Student reported perimeter as area.\n2. P = 32; A = 15. Student reported perimeter as area.\n3. P = 38; A = 60. Student reported perimeter as area.\n4. P = 32; A = 60. Student reported perimeter as area.\n5. P = 40; A = 100. Student reported perimeter as area.\n6. P = 36; A = 56. Student reported perimeter as area.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Polygons and Perimeter Connections” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to dropping a negative sign when comparing…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Polygons and Perimeter Connections.” Use one concrete numeric example with 7 and 4. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
