    objectives: "• Reflect across axes; describe coordinate changes.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Reflecting Points\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 9 of 11 · Coordinate Plane · Lesson 3*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** reflect across axes; describe coordinate changes.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nLook at these two quantities from a family road trip on I-20: 7 and 6. What is one mathematical question you could ask about how they relate?\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 9 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Reflecting Points** — sits inside coordinate plane. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a family road trip on I-20. Related quantities show up as 7 and 6 (and sometimes a third measure 34).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 7 and 6. One useful related value is 13; another is a scaled pair such as 14 to 12 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a family road trip on I-20?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 7 to 6. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 40, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Reflecting Points” but switch the context to a church youth group picnic. Use numbers 7, 9, and 34.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** confusing surface area (wrapping) with volume (filling).\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nGeometry measures need correct units and clear diagrams. Algebraic expressions often summarize geometric patterns.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 7 and 6. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 7 and 6 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 7 and 34. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a family road trip on I-20).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “42.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Reflect (5, 0) across the x-axis and across the y-axis. Give both image coordinates.\n2. Reflect (7, 6) across the x-axis and across the y-axis. Give both image coordinates.\n3. Reflect (0, 5) across the x-axis and across the y-axis. Give both image coordinates.\n4. Reflect (2, 4) across the x-axis and across the y-axis. Give both image coordinates.\n5. Reflect (4, 10) across the x-axis and across the y-axis. Give both image coordinates.\n6. Reflect (6, 9) across the x-axis and across the y-axis. Give both image coordinates.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. Across x-axis: (5, 0). Across y-axis: (-5, 0).\n2. Across x-axis: (7, -6). Across y-axis: (-7, 6).\n3. Across x-axis: (0, -5). Across y-axis: (0, 5).\n4. Across x-axis: (2, -4). Across y-axis: (-2, 4).\n5. Across x-axis: (4, -10). Across y-axis: (-4, 10).\n6. Across x-axis: (6, -9). Across y-axis: (-6, 9).\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Reflecting Points” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to confusing surface area (wrapping) with volume…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Reflecting Points.” Use one concrete numeric example with 7 and 6. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
