    objectives: "• Use SA = 2lw + 2lh + 2wh with meaning.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Surface Area Formula for Rectangular Prisms\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 10 of 11 · 3D Figures · Lesson 4*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** use SA = 2lw + 2lh + 2wh with meaning.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout calculating yet, estimate which is greater: 3/10 of 29, or 60% of 29? Jot a one-sentence guess.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 10 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Surface Area Formula for Rectangular Prisms** — sits inside 3d figures. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a church youth group picnic. Related quantities show up as 3 and 10 (and sometimes a third measure 29).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 3 and 10. One useful related value is 13; another is a scaled pair such as 6 to 20 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a church youth group picnic?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 3 to 10. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 9, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Surface Area Formula for Rectangular Prisms” but switch the context to an East Texas trail hike near Tyler. Use numbers 6, 24, and 29.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** calling a non-statistical question 'statistical' because it uses a number.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nGeometry measures need correct units and clear diagrams. Algebraic expressions often summarize geometric patterns.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 3 and 10. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 3 and 10 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 6 and 29. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a church youth group picnic).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “30.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Use SA = 2lw+2lh+2wh for l=7, w=11, h=6. Show each pair of faces.\n2. Use SA = 2lw+2lh+2wh for l=7, w=6, h=8. Show each pair of faces.\n3. Use SA = 2lw+2lh+2wh for l=7, w=10, h=9. Show each pair of faces.\n4. Use SA = 2lw+2lh+2wh for l=7, w=13, h=10. Show each pair of faces.\n5. Use SA = 2lw+2lh+2wh for l=16, w=8, h=12. Show each pair of faces.\n6. Use SA = 2lw+2lh+2wh for l=16, w=12, h=13. Show each pair of faces.\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. 2(7*11)+2(7*6)+2(11*6) = 370.\n2. 2(7*6)+2(7*8)+2(6*8) = 292.\n3. 2(7*10)+2(7*9)+2(10*9) = 446.\n4. 2(7*13)+2(7*10)+2(13*10) = 582.\n5. 2(16*8)+2(16*12)+2(8*12) = 832.\n6. 2(16*12)+2(16*13)+2(12*13) = 1112.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Surface Area Formula for Rectangular Prisms” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to calling a non-statistical question 'statistical' because…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Surface Area Formula for Rectangular Prisms.” Use one concrete numeric example with 3 and 10. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
