    content: "# Dot Plots\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 11 of 11 · Data and Statistics · Lesson 3*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** build and read dot plots; describe clusters.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nLook at these two quantities from an East Texas trail hike near Tyler: 7 and -2. What is one mathematical question you could ask about how they relate?\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 11 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Dot Plots** — sits inside data and statistics. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine an East Texas trail hike near Tyler. Related quantities show up as 7 and -2 (and sometimes a third measure 48).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 7 and -2. One useful related value is 5; another is a scaled pair such as 14 to -4 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for an East Texas trail hike near Tyler?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 7 to -2. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near -168, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Dot Plots” but switch the context to a scholarship fundraising bake sale. Use numbers 4, 23, and 48.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** treating percent as 'per ten' instead of 'per hundred'.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nStatistics begins with questions that anticipate variability — displays and measures of center come after the question is clear.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 7 and -2. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 7 and -2 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 4 and 48. Explain why your representation matches the situation (an East Texas trail hike near Tyler).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “-14.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Data (hours of homework): 8, 4, 8, 9, 3, 8. Sketch a letter-style dot plot description (list stack heights per value). Where is a cluster?\n2. Data (hours of homework): 8, 7, 9, 9, 6, 7. Sketch a letter-style dot plot description (list stack heights per value). Where is a cluster?\n3. Data (hours of homework): 8, 11, 10, 9, 10, 6. Sketch a letter-style dot plot description (list stack heights per value). Where is a cluster?\n4. Data (hours of homework): 8, 14, 11, 9, 13, 5. Sketch a letter-style dot plot description (list stack heights per value). Where is a cluster?\n5. Data (hours of homework): 8, 9, 13, 9, 8, 24. Sketch a letter-style dot plot description (list stack heights per value). Where is a cluster?\n6. Data (hours of homework): 8, 13, 14, 9, 12, 23. Sketch a letter-style dot plot description (list stack heights per value). Where is a cluster?\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. Count frequency per distinct value in [8, 4, 8, 9, 3, 8]; cluster = values with most dots.\n2. Count frequency per distinct value in [8, 7, 9, 9, 6, 7]; cluster = values with most dots.\n3. Count frequency per distinct value in [8, 11, 10, 9, 10, 6]; cluster = values with most dots.\n4. Count frequency per distinct value in [8, 14, 11, 9, 13, 5]; cluster = values with most dots.\n5. Count frequency per distinct value in [8, 9, 13, 9, 8, 24]; cluster = values with most dots.\n6. Count frequency per distinct value in [8, 13, 14, 9, 12, 23]; cluster = values with most dots.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Dot Plots” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to treating percent as 'per ten' instead…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Dot Plots.” Use one concrete numeric example with 7 and -2. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
