    objectives: "• Compute and interpret the mean.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Measures of Center: Mean\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 11 of 11 · Data and Statistics · Lesson 5*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** compute and interpret the mean.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nWithout calculating yet, estimate which is greater: 8/4 of 37, or 30% of 37? Jot a one-sentence guess.\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 11 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Measures of Center: Mean** — sits inside data and statistics. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a school garden plot. Related quantities show up as 8 and 4 (and sometimes a third measure 37).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 8 and 4. One useful related value is 12; another is a scaled pair such as 16 to 8 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a school garden plot?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 8 to 4. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 74, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Measures of Center: Mean” but switch the context to a band concert ticket table. Use numbers 3, 12, and 37.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** dropping a negative sign when comparing signed numbers.\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nStatistics begins with questions that anticipate variability — displays and measures of center come after the question is clear.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 8 and 4. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 8 and 4 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 3 and 37. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a school garden plot).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “32.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Find the mean of 10, 10, 7, 11, 9, 21. Show sum ÷ count. What does the mean represent?\n2. Find the mean of 8, 10, 9, 9, 9, 18. Show sum ÷ count. What does the mean represent?\n3. Find the mean of 10, 9, 10, 11, 8, 19. Show sum ÷ count. What does the mean represent?\n4. Find the mean of 7, 14, 9, 8, 13, 24. Show sum ÷ count. What does the mean represent?\n5. Find the mean of 9, 13, 10, 10, 12, 5. Show sum ÷ count. What does the mean represent?\n6. Find the mean of 16, 13, 12, 17, 12, 22. Show sum ÷ count. What does the mean represent?\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. Mean = 68/6 = 11.33. Fair-share / balance point.\n2. Mean = 63/6 = 10.50. Fair-share / balance point.\n3. Mean = 67/6 = 11.17. Fair-share / balance point.\n4. Mean = 75/6 = 12.50. Fair-share / balance point.\n5. Mean = 59/6 = 9.83. Fair-share / balance point.\n6. Mean = 92/6 = 15.33. Fair-share / balance point.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Measures of Center: Mean” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to dropping a negative sign when comparing…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Measures of Center: Mean.” Use one concrete numeric example with 8 and 4. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
