    objectives: "• Find medians; compare mean vs median briefly.\n• Explain the idea with a representation (table, diagram, number line, or expression).\n• Check work with an estimate, inverse operation, or second representation.",
    content: "# Measures of Center: Median\n\n*Grade 6 Mathematics · Unit 11 of 11 · Data and Statistics · Lesson 6*\n\n## Objective\n\n**I can** find medians; compare mean vs median briefly.\n\n**Teacher focus:** Students explain *why* a representation works, not only how to compute. Prefer labeled diagrams and complete sentences on exit tickets.\n\n## Warm-up (3–5 minutes)\n\nSketch a tiny diagram (table, number line, or shape) that might help with: \"Find medians; compare mean vs median briefly.\"\n\nShare with a partner: What prior skill from earlier in Unit 11 (or a previous unit) might help?\n\n## Teach\n\n### Big idea\n\nToday's skill — **Measures of Center: Median** — sits inside data and statistics. In Grade 6 we build flexible representations: words, tables, diagrams, number lines, and symbols. A correct answer with no representation is weaker evidence of understanding than a clear diagram plus a checked result.\n\n### Why it matters\n\nFamilies use these ideas when comparing prices, reading sports stats, planning travel time, scaling recipes, or tracking fundraising goals. Connect careful quantitative reasoning to scholarship habits: check units, estimate first, and explain your thinking so a teacher (or future you) can follow it.\n\n### Language bank\n\n- **Precise words beat vague words.** Prefer “unit rate,” “equivalent,” “perpendicular height,” “absolute value,” or “like terms” when those are the ideas in play.\n- **Order matters** in ratios and ordered pairs; **operation order** matters in numerical expressions.\n- **Units tell a story.** “3” is incomplete; “3 miles per hour” or “3 square feet” carries meaning.\n\n### Worked example A\n\nImagine a library reading challenge. Related quantities show up as 9 and 2 (and sometimes a third measure 10).\n\n**Step 1 — Restate the question.** What are we finding, and in what units?\n**Step 2 — Choose a representation.** For ratios/rates: table or double number line. For signed numbers: number line. For area: labeled sketch. For expressions: define the variable in a sentence.\n**Step 3 — Compute with the representation visible.** Example scaffold numbers: start from 9 and 2. One useful related value is 11; another is a scaled pair such as 18 to 4 when equivalence is the goal.\n**Step 4 — Check.** Use an inverse operation, an estimate, or a second representation. Ask: Is the answer reasonable for a library reading challenge?\n\n**Sample narrative solution (model quality, not the only path):**\n“I compared 9 to 2. I built a small table by scaling both numbers by 2 and by 3. The equivalent pairs stayed in the same relationship. My estimate using friendly numbers was near 45, which matched my more precise result closely enough that I trust the work.”\n\n### Worked example B (different structure)\n\nNow change the story: keep the mathematical structure of “Measures of Center: Median” but switch the context to a Saturday soccer tournament. Use numbers 0, 5, and 10.\n\n1. Write a one-sentence goal.\n2. Draw the representation.\n3. Compute.\n4. Write a concluding sentence that includes units.\n\nTeachers: freeze after step 2 in live sessions so students cannot hide behind premature arithmetic.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Watch for:** confusing surface area (wrapping) with volume (filling).\n- **Also watch for:** skipping the estimate, then accepting an impossible result (negative lengths, percents over 100% when the story forbids it, or areas labeled with linear units).\n- **Repair move:** Name the broken step in one sentence, then re-do only from that step — do not erase the entire solution blindly.\n\n### Connect to prior learning\n\nStatistics begins with questions that anticipate variability — displays and measures of center come after the question is clear.\n\n## Guided practice (we do)\n\n1. **Guided 1 — Use today's idea on numbers 9 and 2. Show each step.**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Work with 9 and 2 using the lesson method; check with an inverse operation or equivalent representation.\n\n1. **Guided 2 — Same structure, new numbers 0 and 10. Explain why your representation matches the situation (a library reading challenge).**\n   - *Teacher/self-check note:* Scale or operate consistently; label units; verify with a second method (table, line, or estimate).\n\n2. **Error hunt:** A fictional student forgot a label and wrote only “18.” What question should you ask them before accepting the number?\n\n## Independent practice\n\nComplete each item with visible work. Aim for clear diagrams + checked answers.\n\n1. Find the median of 7, 5, 7, 8, 4, 22. Order first. When would median be more helpful than mean?\n2. Find the median of 11, 7, 7, 12, 6, 5. Order first. When would median be more helpful than mean?\n3. Find the median of 11, 10, 8, 12, 9, 24. Order first. When would median be more helpful than mean?\n4. Find the median of 6, 12, 8, 7, 11, 7. Order first. When would median be more helpful than mean?\n5. Find the median of 15, 8, 10, 16, 7, 6. Order first. When would median be more helpful than mean?\n6. Find the median of 10, 10, 10, 11, 9, 9. Order first. When would median be more helpful than mean?\n\n### Answer key (try first)\n\n*Check your work only after you attempt each item. Show your representation, not only the final number.*\n\n1. Ordered 4, 5, 7, 7, 8, 22; median 7. Median resists extreme outliers better than mean.\n2. Ordered 5, 6, 7, 7, 11, 12; median 7. Median resists extreme outliers better than mean.\n3. Ordered 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 24; median 10.5. Median resists extreme outliers better than mean.\n4. Ordered 6, 7, 7, 8, 11, 12; median 7.5. Median resists extreme outliers better than mean.\n5. Ordered 6, 7, 8, 10, 15, 16; median 9. Median resists extreme outliers better than mean.\n6. Ordered 9, 9, 10, 10, 10, 11; median 10. Median resists extreme outliers better than mean.\n\n## Exit ticket\n\n1. In one sentence, what does “Measures of Center: Median” let you do that you could not do as clearly before?\n2. Name the common mistake to avoid today (hint: related to confusing surface area (wrapping) with volume…).\n3. Create one new practice item (with answer) a classmate could try in 2 minutes.\n\n*(Scored exit items also appear as multiple-choice checks below the lesson in Prosper Prep.)*\n\n## Stretch (optional)\n\nWrite a short CER paragraph (4–6 sentences) arguing that a clear representation improves trust in an answer for “Measures of Center: Median.” Use one concrete numeric example with 9 and 2. Then invent a harder variant that would challenge a classmate who already finished the independent set.\n",
